;; What the in-app native bridge may do, evaluated inside the app.
;;
;; Nothing. The surface this app ships today reads a public catalog over HTTPS
;; and stores nothing, so it needs no clipboard, no app-data filesystem, no
;; keychain, no notifications and not even the bridge's own `http/fetch` — an
;; ordinary browser fetch reaches the edge, and routing it through a native
;; provider would trade CORS for a grant that can reach ANY host: the policy
;; names commands, not destinations (kotoba.shell.launcher/policy-decision).
;;
;; `:deny ["*"]` beside the empty allow list is not redundant. `policy-decision`
;; already refuses what is not allowed, so this states the same thing a second
;; way — and it is the state the native halves fall back to when the policy
;; asset cannot be read at all, so an app whose asset is missing behaves like
;; this one rather than differently from it.
;;
;; When a slice needs a capability, it is one string here plus the reason it is
;; needed. Adding one is a decision; leaving one in after the need is gone is
;; how a read-only app ends up holding a keychain.
{:allow []
 :deny ["*"]
 :audit true}
